<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - HW1, online version: the same four problems as the written    -->
<!-- Assignment 1, rebuilt as auto-graded exercises.                           -->
<!--                                                                         -->
<!-- Each blank is a <var/> in the statement, matched in order by a <var> in  -->
<!-- <setup>.  The first <condition> of a var is the correct answer; the      -->
<!-- others catch a likely mistake and explain it.  Runestone scores every    -->
<!-- blank on its own, so students keep the credit for the parts they get     -->
<!-- right.  (This is the "fillin-basic" flavour, which renders inline in a   -->
<!-- plain HTML build; the newer <fillin>/<evaluation> flavour needs the      -->
<!-- dynamic runtime and shows an empty box without it.)                      -->
<section xml:id="sec-exercises-hw1-online" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Assignment 1</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Sections: <xref ref="sec-parametrization" text="title"/> and
      <xref ref="sec-arc-length" text="title"/>. Work each problem, then type your answers into the boxes and have them checked here.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each problem below has several answer boxes, and <em>every box is
      graded separately</em>: a box you fill in correctly earns its credit
      even if another box in the same problem is wrong. Press
      <q>Check Me</q> to submit; you may check as often as you like.
    </p>
    <p>
      <term>How to type your answers.</term>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Numbers.</term> Type a decimal number and nothing else: no
          units, no <m>=</m> sign, no <m>\pi</m>. Give at least three
          decimal places (for instance <c>132.008</c>, not <c>132</c> and
          not <c>42pi</c>). Answers are accepted within a small tolerance,
          so a little rounding is fine.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Expressions.</term> Type only the expression itself, not
          <c>z(t) =</c>. Write products with <c>*</c> and powers with
          <c>^</c>, and write functions with parentheses:
          <c>cos(t)</c>, <c>sin(t)</c>, <c>ln(t)</c>, <c>sqrt(t)</c>. So the
          expression <m>4\cos^2(t)</m> is typed <c>4cos(t)^2</c>. Spaces are
          ignored.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </introduction>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-hw1-online">

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <!-- Problems 38 and 41 and Example 7 of the textbook's "Using Time as   -->
  <!-- the Parameter" material.                                            -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw1o-motion-i">
    <title>Describing a Motion, I</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        An object moves along a curve so that at time <m>t</m> it has
        coordinates
        <me>x(t) = \frac{3t}{\sqrt{t^2+1}}, \qquad
            y(t) = \frac{3}{\sqrt{t^2+1}}.</me>
        Describe the motion.
      </p>
      <p>
        Identify the curve the object travels on: it
        is a <var width="10"/> (type <c>line</c>, <c>parabola</c>, <c>circle</c>,
        <c>ellipse</c>, or <c>hyperbola</c>).
      </p>
      <p>
        Then graph the path and indicate the direction of motion. There is
        no box for this part: include the graph in the solution you submit
        to the grader.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="circle">
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="ellipse">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw1-solutions">
      <p>
        For every <m>t</m>,
        <me>x^2 + y^2 = \frac{9t^2}{t^2+1} + \frac{9}{t^2+1}
            = \frac{9(t^2+1)}{t^2+1} = 9,</me>
        so the object stays on the circle of radius <m>3</m> centered at the
        origin. Since <m>y(t) = 3/\sqrt{t^2+1} > 0</m> for every <m>t</m>,
        only the <em>upper</em> half of that circle is ever visited.
      </p>
      <p>
        As <m>t \to -\infty</m>, <m>x \to -3</m> and <m>y \to 0</m>, so the
        object comes in from near <m>(-3,0)</m>; at <m>t = 0</m> it is at
        <m>(0,3)</m>, the top of the circle; and as <m>t \to \infty</m> it
        approaches <m>(3,0)</m>. Because
        <m>x'(t) = 3/(t^2+1)^{3/2} > 0</m>, the <m>x</m>-coordinate always
        increases, so the object moves steadily from left to right over the top
        of the circle: <em>clockwise</em>, as in
        <xref ref="fig-hw1o-semicircle"/>. It never reaches the two endpoints
        <m>(\pm 3, 0)</m>; it only approaches them.
      </p>
      <p>
        The speed is <m>\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\rVert = 3/(t^2+1)</m>: the
        object is fastest at the top, at <m>t=0</m>, and slows down as it
        nears either endpoint.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-hw1o-semicircle">
        <caption>
          The path of <xref ref="ex-hw1o-motion-i"/>: the upper half
          of the circle <m>x^2+y^2=9</m>, traced clockwise.
        </caption>
        <image width="70%">
          <shortdescription>
          The upper semicircle of radius three, traced from left to right, with open dots at its two endpoints.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
            The upper half of the circle of radius three centered at the origin,
            drawn in blue with an arrowhead showing motion from left to right.
            Open dots at minus three comma zero and three comma zero mark the
            endpoints the object approaches but never reaches; red dots mark the
            positions at t equals minus one, t equals zero (the top point zero
            comma three), and t equals one.
            </p>
          </description>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-hw1o-semicircle">
            <diagram dimensions="(420,270)" margins="5">
              <definition>c(s) = (3*sin(s), 3*cos(s))</definition>
              <definition>q(t) = (3*t/sqrt(t^2+1), 3/sqrt(t^2+1))</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-5.2,-1.1,5.2,4.1)" aspect-ratio="1">
                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                <group at="curve">
                  <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(-pi/2,pi/2)" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                  <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(0.5,0.7)" stroke="blue"
                                    thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                </group>
                <group at="ends">
                  <point p="(-3,0)" size="4.5" stroke="blue" fill="white"/>
                  <label anchor="(-3,0)" alignment="nw" offset="(-4,8)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>t\to-\infty</m>
                  </label>
                  <point p="(3,0)" size="4.5" stroke="blue" fill="white"/>
                  <label anchor="(3,0)" alignment="ne" offset="(4,8)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>t\to\infty</m>
                  </label>
                </group>
                <group at="marks">
                  <point p="q(-1)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                  <label anchor="q(-1)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,6)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>t=-1</m>
                  </label>
                  <point p="q(0)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                  <label anchor="q(0)" alignment="n" offset="(0,8)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>t=0</m>
                  </label>
                  <point p="q(1)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                  <label anchor="q(1)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,6)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>t=1</m>
                  </label>
                </group>
              </coordinates>
              <annotations>
                <annotation ref="figure" text="The upper half of the circle of radius three centered at the origin, traced clockwise from left to right">
                  <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                  <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                  <annotation ref="curve" text="The upper semicircle of radius three, traced from left to right, that is, clockwise" sonify="yes"/>
                  <annotation ref="ends" text="Open dots at minus three comma zero and three comma zero, the two endpoints the object approaches as t goes to minus infinity and to infinity but never reaches"/>
                  <annotation ref="marks" text="Three dots marking the positions at t equals minus one, t equals zero at the top point zero comma three, and t equals one"/>
                </annotation>
              </annotations>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw1o-motion-ii">
    <title>Describing a Motion, II</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        An object moves along a curve so that at time <m>t</m> it has
        coordinates
        <me>x(t) = \sin t - 2, \qquad y(t) = 4 - 2\cos t, \qquad
            0 \le t \le 2\pi.</me>
        Describe the motion.
      </p>
      <p>
        Identify the curve the object travels on: it
        is a <var width="10"/> (type <c>line</c>, <c>parabola</c>, <c>circle</c>,
        <c>ellipse</c>, or <c>hyperbola</c>).
      </p>
      <p>
        Then graph the path and indicate the direction of motion. There is
        no box for this part: include the graph in the solution you submit
        to the grader.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="ellipse">
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="circle">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw1-solutions">
      <p>
        From <m>x = \sin t - 2</m> and <m>y = 4 - 2\cos t</m> we get
        <m>\sin t = x + 2</m> and <m>\cos t = (4 - y)/2</m>, so
        <me>(x+2)^2 + \frac{(y-4)^2}{4} = \sin^2 t + \cos^2 t = 1.</me>
        The path is the ellipse centered at <m>(-2,4)</m> that extends <m>1</m>
        unit left and right of the center and <m>2</m> units above and below it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Following the object through one period:
        at <m>t=0</m> it is at <m>(-2,2)</m>, the bottom of the ellipse;
        at <m>t=\pi/2</m> at <m>(-1,4)</m>, the rightmost point;
        at <m>t=\pi</m> at <m>(-2,6)</m>, the top;
        at <m>t=3\pi/2</m> at <m>(-3,4)</m>, the leftmost point;
        and at <m>t=2\pi</m> it is back at <m>(-2,2)</m>.
        Bottom, right, top, left is <em>counterclockwise</em>, so as <m>t</m>
        runs from <m>0</m> to <m>2\pi</m> the object goes once around the
        ellipse counterclockwise, starting and ending at its lowest point
        (<xref ref="fig-hw1o-ellipse-motion"/>).
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-hw1o-ellipse-motion">
        <caption>
          The path of <xref ref="ex-hw1o-motion-ii"/>: the ellipse
          <m>(x+2)^2 + \dfrac{(y-4)^2}{4} = 1</m>, traced once counterclockwise
          from its lowest point.
        </caption>
        <image width="50%">
          <shortdescription>
          An ellipse centered at (-2,4), taller than it is wide, traced counterclockwise starting from its bottom point.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
            An ellipse centered at minus two comma four, one unit wide on each
            side of the center and two units tall on each side, drawn in blue
            with an arrowhead showing counterclockwise motion. Red dots mark the
            positions at t equals zero (bottom), pi over two (right), pi (top),
            and three pi over two (left).
            </p>
          </description>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-hw1o-ellipse-motion">
            <diagram dimensions="(320,416)" margins="5">
              <definition>c(t) = (sin(t) - 2, 4 - 2*cos(t))</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-4.6,-0.8,1.4,7.0)" aspect-ratio="1">
                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                <group at="curve">
                  <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(0,2*pi)" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                  <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(3.5,3.9)" stroke="blue"
                                    thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                </group>
                <group at="center">
                  <point p="(-2,4)" size="3.5" stroke="black" fill="black"/>
                  <label anchor="(-2,4)" alignment="ne" offset="(5,4)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>(-2,4)</m>
                  </label>
                </group>
                <group at="quarters">
                  <point p="c(0)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                  <label anchor="c(0)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-8)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>t=0</m>
                  </label>
                  <point p="c(pi/2)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                  <label anchor="c(pi/2)" alignment="e" offset="(8,0)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>t=\pi/2</m>
                  </label>
                  <point p="c(pi)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                  <label anchor="c(pi)" alignment="n" offset="(0,8)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>t=\pi</m>
                  </label>
                  <point p="c(3*pi/2)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                  <label anchor="c(3*pi/2)" alignment="w" offset="(-8,0)" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>t=3\pi/2</m>
                  </label>
                </group>
              </coordinates>
              <annotations>
                <annotation ref="figure" text="An ellipse centered at minus two comma four, one unit wide on each side of the center and two units tall on each side, traced once counterclockwise starting from its lowest point">
                  <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                  <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                  <annotation ref="curve" text="The ellipse, traced counterclockwise" sonify="yes"/>
                  <annotation ref="center" text="The center of the ellipse, at minus two comma four"/>
                  <annotation ref="quarters" text="Four dots marking the positions at t equals zero, at the bottom point minus two comma two; t equals pi over two, at the right point minus one comma four; t equals pi, at the top point minus two comma six; and t equals three pi over two, at the left point minus three comma four"/>
                </annotation>
              </annotations>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw1o-ellipse-parametrize">
    <title>Parametrizing an Ellipse as a Motion</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find parametric equations that trace out the ellipse
        <me>\frac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1,</me>
        where the parameter <m>t</m> is time (in seconds), in each of the
        two ways below. Write <m>\pi</m> as <c>pi</c>: for instance,
        <m>3\pi t</m> is typed <c>3pi t</c> or <c>3*pi*t</c>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(a)</term> The motion around the ellipse is counterclockwise,
        begins at the point <m>(2,0)</m>, and requires <m>1</m> second for
        a complete revolution.
      </p>
      <p><m>x(t) =</m> <var width="14"/></p>
      <p><m>y(t) =</m> <var width="14"/></p>
      <p>
        One complete revolution is <m>0 \le t \le</m> <var width="5"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(b)</term> The motion around the ellipse is clockwise, begins
        at the point <m>(0,1)</m>, and requires <m>2</m> seconds for a
        complete revolution.
      </p>
      <p><m>x(t) =</m> <var width="14"/></p>
      <p><m>y(t) =</m> <var width="14"/></p>
      <p>
        One complete revolution is <m>0 \le t \le</m> <var width="5"/>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="2\s*\*?\s*cos\s*\(\s*2\s*\*?\s*(pi|π)\s*\*?\s*t\s*\)">
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="2\s*\*?\s*sin\s*\(\s*2\s*\*?\s*(pi|π)\s*\*?\s*t\s*\)">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="sin\s*\(\s*2\s*\*?\s*(pi|π)\s*\*?\s*t\s*\)">
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="cos\s*\(\s*2\s*\*?\s*(pi|π)\s*\*?\s*t\s*\)">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="1" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="6.283" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="2\s*\*?\s*sin\s*\(\s*((pi|π)\s*\*?\s*t|t\s*\*?\s*(pi|π))\s*\)">
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="2\s*\*?\s*cos\s*\(\s*((pi|π)\s*\*?\s*t|t\s*\*?\s*(pi|π))\s*\)">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="cos\s*\(\s*((pi|π)\s*\*?\s*t|t\s*\*?\s*(pi|π))\s*\)">
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="sin\s*\(\s*((pi|π)\s*\*?\s*t|t\s*\*?\s*(pi|π))\s*\)">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="2" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="6.283" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
            <p>Incorrect.</p>
          </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw1-solutions">
      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-hw1o-ellipse"/> shows the ellipse with the starting
        point of each motion marked.
      </p>
      <figure xml:id="fig-hw1o-ellipse">
        <caption>
          The ellipse <m>\dfrac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1</m>, with the starting point
          of each motion marked.
        </caption>
        <image width="70%">
          <shortdescription>
          The ellipse x squared over four plus y squared equals one, with the points (2,0) and (0,1) marked.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
            An ellipse centered at the origin, crossing the x axis at plus and
            minus two and the y axis at plus and minus one. The point two comma
            zero is marked in orange and the point zero comma one in green.
            </p>
          </description>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-hw1o-ellipse">
            <diagram dimensions="(462,273)" margins="5">
              <definition>c(t) = (2*cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-3.1,-2.0,3.5,1.9)" aspect-ratio="1">
                <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                <group at="curve">
                  <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(0,2*pi)" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                </group>
                <group at="starts">
                  <point p="(2,0)" size="4.5" stroke="#D55E00" fill="#D55E00"/>
                  <label anchor="(2,0)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,6)" color="#D55E00" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>(2,0)</m>
                  </label>
                  <point p="(0,1)" size="4.5" stroke="#009E73" fill="#009E73"/>
                  <label anchor="(0,1)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,6)" color="#009E73" clear-background="yes">
                    <m>(0,1)</m>
                  </label>
                </group>
              </coordinates>
              <annotations>
                <annotation ref="figure" text="The ellipse x squared over four plus y squared equals one, which crosses the x axis at plus and minus two and the y axis at plus and minus one">
                  <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                  <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                  <annotation ref="curve" text="The ellipse" sonify="yes"/>
                  <annotation ref="starts" text="Two marked points: two comma zero, where the motion in part a begins, and zero comma one, where the motion in part b begins"/>
                </annotation>
              </annotations>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </figure>
      <p>
        <term>(a)</term> The equations <m>x = 2\cos(\omega t)</m>,
        <m>y = \sin(\omega t)</m> satisfy
        <m>x^2/4 + y^2 = \cos^2(\omega t) + \sin^2(\omega t) = 1</m>, start
        at <m>(2,0)</m> when <m>t=0</m>, and move counterclockwise, since just
        after <m>t=0</m> the point has <m>y > 0</m> and <m>x</m> decreasing. One
        revolution takes <m>2\pi/\omega</m> seconds, and we want this to be
        <m>1</m>, so <m>\omega = 2\pi</m>:
        <me>x(t) = 2\cos(2\pi t), \qquad y(t) = \sin(2\pi t), \qquad
            0 \le t \le 1.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(b)</term> To start at <m>(0,1)</m>, swap the roles of sine and
        cosine: <m>x = 2\sin(\omega t)</m>, <m>y = \cos(\omega t)</m> is at
        <m>(0,1)</m> when <m>t=0</m>, and just afterwards <m>x</m> is positive
        and <m>y</m> is decreasing, so the point heads toward <m>(2,0)</m>:
        clockwise. A revolution takes <m>2\pi/\omega = 2</m> seconds, so
        <m>\omega = \pi</m>:
        <me>x(t) = 2\sin(\pi t), \qquad y(t) = \cos(\pi t), \qquad
            0 \le t \le 2.</me>
      </p>
      <p>
        <xref ref="fig-hw1o-ellipse-ccw"/> and
        <xref ref="fig-hw1o-ellipse-cw"/> show the two motions, with the
        position marked every quarter of a revolution.
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="0%" valign="middle">
        <figure xml:id="fig-hw1o-ellipse-ccw">
          <caption>
            Part (a): counterclockwise from <m>(2,0)</m>, one revolution per
            second.
          </caption>
          <image width="100%">
            <shortdescription>
            The ellipse traced counterclockwise from (2,0), with the quarter-second positions marked.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
              The ellipse x squared over four plus y squared equals one, drawn in
              blue with an arrowhead showing counterclockwise motion. Red dots mark
              the positions at t equals zero and one, at two comma zero; t equals
              one quarter, at zero comma one; t equals one half, at minus two
              comma zero; and t equals three quarters, at zero comma minus one.
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-hw1o-ellipse-ccw">
              <diagram dimensions="(462,273)" margins="5">
                <definition>c(t) = (2*cos(2*pi*t), sin(2*pi*t))</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-3.1,-2.0,3.5,1.9)" aspect-ratio="1">
                  <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                  <group at="curve">
                    <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(0,1)" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                    <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(0.08,0.14)" stroke="blue"
                                      thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                  </group>
                  <group at="quarters">
                    <point p="c(0)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                    <label anchor="c(0)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,8)" clear-background="yes">
                      <m>t=0,\ 1</m>
                    </label>
                    <point p="c(0.25)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                    <label anchor="c(0.25)" alignment="n" offset="(0,8)" clear-background="yes">
                      <m>t=\tfrac14</m>
                    </label>
                    <point p="c(0.5)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                    <label anchor="c(0.5)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,6)" clear-background="yes">
                      <m>t=\tfrac12</m>
                    </label>
                    <point p="c(0.75)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                    <label anchor="c(0.75)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-8)" clear-background="yes">
                      <m>t=\tfrac34</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure" text="The ellipse traced counterclockwise, starting at two comma zero and returning there after one second">
                    <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                    <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                    <annotation ref="curve" text="The ellipse, traced counterclockwise" sonify="yes"/>
                    <annotation ref="quarters" text="Four dots marking the positions at t equals zero and one, at two comma zero; t equals one quarter, at zero comma one; t equals one half, at minus two comma zero; and t equals three quarters, at zero comma minus one"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
        <figure xml:id="fig-hw1o-ellipse-cw">
          <caption>
            Part (b): clockwise from <m>(0,1)</m>, one revolution every two
            seconds.
          </caption>
          <image width="100%">
            <shortdescription>
            The ellipse traced clockwise from (0,1), with the half-second positions marked.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
              The same ellipse, drawn in blue with an arrowhead showing clockwise
              motion. Red dots mark the positions at t equals zero and two, at
              zero comma one; t equals one half, at two comma zero; t equals one,
              at zero comma minus one; and t equals three halves, at minus two
              comma zero.
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-hw1o-ellipse-cw">
              <diagram dimensions="(462,273)" margins="5">
                <definition>c(t) = (2*sin(pi*t), cos(pi*t))</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-3.1,-2.0,3.5,1.9)" aspect-ratio="1">
                  <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                  <group at="curve">
                    <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(0,2)" stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                    <parametric-curve function="c" domain="(0.25,0.38)" stroke="blue"
                                      thickness="3" arrows="1"/>
                  </group>
                  <group at="quarters">
                    <point p="c(0)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                    <label anchor="c(0)" alignment="ne" offset="(8,6)" clear-background="yes">
                      <m>t=0,\ 2</m>
                    </label>
                    <point p="c(0.5)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                    <label anchor="c(0.5)" alignment="ne" offset="(6,6)" clear-background="yes">
                      <m>t=\tfrac12</m>
                    </label>
                    <point p="c(1)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                    <label anchor="c(1)" alignment="s" offset="(0,-8)" clear-background="yes">
                      <m>t=1</m>
                    </label>
                    <point p="c(1.5)" size="4" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                    <label anchor="c(1.5)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,6)" clear-background="yes">
                      <m>t=\tfrac32</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>
                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure" text="The ellipse traced clockwise, starting at zero comma one and returning there after two seconds">
                    <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                    <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                    <annotation ref="curve" text="The ellipse, traced clockwise" sonify="yes"/>
                    <annotation ref="quarters" text="Four dots marking the positions at t equals zero and two, at zero comma one; t equals one half, at two comma zero; t equals one, at zero comma minus one; and t equals three halves, at minus two comma zero"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </sidebyside>
    </solution>
  </exercise>


  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw1o-parametrize-intersection">
    <title>Parametrizing a Curve of Intersection</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The curve <m>C</m> is the intersection of the cylinder
        <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m> and the surface <m>z = 4x^2</m>. Parametrize
        <m>C</m> by filling in the three components below, using the
        standard parametrization of the unit circle for the shadow of
        <m>C</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane.
      </p>
      <p><m>x(t) =</m> <var width="14"/></p>
      <p><m>y(t) =</m> <var width="14"/></p>
      <p><m>z(t) =</m> <var width="14"/></p>
      <p>
        The parameter <m>t</m> runs from <m>0</m> to <var width="8"/>,
        which traces <m>C</m> exactly once.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="cos\s*\(\s*t\s*\)">
          <feedback>
   <p>Correct.</p>
 </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="sin\s*\(\s*t\s*\)">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="sin\s*\(\s*t\s*\)">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="4\s*\*?\s*(cos\s*\(\s*t\s*\)\s*\^\s*2|cos\s*\^\s*2\s*\(\s*t\s*\))">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="4\s*\*?\s*(sin\s*\(\s*t\s*\)\s*\^\s*2|sin\s*\^\s*2\s*\(\s*t\s*\))">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="cos\s*\(\s*t\s*\)\s*\^\s*2">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="6.283" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="3.142" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw1-solutions">
      <p>
        The shadow of <m>C</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane is the unit circle, so
        take <m>x=\cos(t)</m>, <m>y=\sin(t)</m> with
        <m>0 \le t \le 2\pi \approx 6.283</m>. Substituting into
        <m>z=4x^2</m> gives <m>z = 4\cos^2(t)</m>, so

      <md>
        \mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle \cos(t),\ \sin(t),\ 4\cos^2(t)
        \right\rangle, \qquad 0 \le t \le 2\pi .
      </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw1o-helix">
    <title>How Much Wire Is in a Spring?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A helix of radius <m>R</m> and height <m>H</m> that makes <m>N</m>
        turns can be parametrized as
      </p>

      <md>
        \mathbf{r}(t)=\left\langle R\cos(t),\ R\sin(t),\ c\,t\right\rangle ,
        \qquad 0 \le t \le T .
      </md>

      <p>
        <term>(a)</term> For spring <term>A</term> of the figure below
        (radius <m>7</m> cm, height <m>4</m> cm, <m>3</m> turns), the
        constant is <m>c =</m> <var width="8"/>
        and the parameter runs up to <m>T =</m> <var width="8"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>(b)</term> Compute the length of each spring, in centimetres.
      </p>
      <p>Length of spring A: <var width="10"/> cm.</p>
      <p>Length of spring B: <var width="10"/> cm.</p>
      <p>
        Which spring uses more wire? Type a single letter, <c>A</c> or
        <c>B</c>: <var width="4"/>
      </p>
    <figure>
      <caption>Which spring uses more wire?</caption>
      <image width="80%">
<shortdescription>
Two coil springs side by side: spring A wide with three turns, spring B narrow with five turns.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
Two helical springs drawn side by side. Spring A, on the left, has
three turns of radius seven centimeters and a marked height of four
centimeters. Spring B, on the right, has five turns of radius four
centimeters and a marked height of three centimeters. Dashed lines
with a double-headed arrow mark each spring's height.
</p>
</description>
        <latex-image>
          <![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.32, line cap=round]
%% Spring A: 3 turns, radius 7, height 4
\begin{scope}
  % wire: outline pass then white core pass
  \draw[line width=3.2pt, black!55]
    plot[domain=90:1170, samples=700, smooth]
    ({7*sin(\x)}, {1.6*cos(\x) + 4*(\x-90)/1080});
  \draw[line width=1.8pt, white]
    plot[domain=90:1170, samples=700, smooth]
    ({7*sin(\x)}, {1.6*cos(\x) + 4*(\x-90)/1080});
  % height marker
  \draw[dashed, black!70] (0,1.6) -- (9.3,1.6);
  \draw[dashed, black!70] (0,5.6) -- (9.3,5.6);
  \draw[<->, black!70] (8.8,1.6) -- (8.8,5.6)
    node[midway, right, black] {\small $4$ cm};
  % caption labels
  \node[align=center] at (0,-4.2) {\small $3$ turns, radius $7$ cm};
  \node at (0,-5.8) {\small (A)};
\end{scope}
%% Spring B: 5 turns, radius 4, height 3
\begin{scope}[xshift=21cm]
  \draw[line width=3.2pt, black!55]
    plot[domain=90:1890, samples=900, smooth]
    ({4*sin(\x)}, {1.0*cos(\x) + 3*(\x-90)/1800});
  \draw[line width=1.8pt, white]
    plot[domain=90:1890, samples=900, smooth]
    ({4*sin(\x)}, {1.0*cos(\x) + 3*(\x-90)/1800});
  \draw[dashed, black!70] (0,1.0) -- (6.3,1.0);
  \draw[dashed, black!70] (0,4.0) -- (6.3,4.0);
  \draw[<->, black!70] (5.8,1.0) -- (5.8,4.0)
    node[midway, right, black] {\small $3$ cm};
  \node[align=center] at (0,-4.2) {\small $5$ turns, radius $4$ cm};
  \node at (0,-5.8) {\small (B)};
\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var>
        <condition number="0.2122" tolerance="0.002">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="1.3333" tolerance="0.002">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="18.850" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="6.283" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="132.008" tolerance="0.05">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="131.947" tolerance="0.02">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="125.700" tolerance="0.05">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="125.664" tolerance="0.02">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var case="insensitive">
        <condition string="a">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition string="b">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw1-solutions">
      <p>
        The height <m>H</m> is gained over <m>N</m> turns, that is, over
        <m>T = 2\pi N</m> units of <m>t</m>, so <m>c = H/(2\pi N)</m>. For
        spring A, <m>c = 4/(6\pi) \approx 0.212</m> and
        <m>T = 6\pi \approx 18.850</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The speed is constant,
        <m>\left\lVert\mathbf{v}\right\rVert = \sqrt{R^2 + c^2}</m>, so

      <md>
        L = \int_0^{2\pi N}\sqrt{R^2+c^2}\,dt
          = \sqrt{\left(2\pi N R\right)^2 + H^2}.
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Spring A gives <m>\sqrt{(42\pi)^2+4^2}\approx 132.008</m> cm and
        spring B gives <m>\sqrt{(40\pi)^2+3^2}\approx 125.700</m> cm, so
        <term>A</term> uses more wire. Notice how little the height
        contributes: the wire is almost all winding.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw1o-tangent-line">
    <title>Tangent Line to a Space Curve</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Consider the curve</p>

      <md>
        \mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle \ln(t),\, \frac{t-1}{t+2},\,
        t\ln(t) \right\rangle
      </md>

      <p>and the point <m>P(0,0,0)</m> on it.</p>
      <p>
        The parameter value at <m>P</m> is <m>t_0 =</m> <var width="6"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The tangent line at <m>P</m> has direction vector
        <m>\mathbf{r}\,'(t_0) = \langle</m> <var width="6"/>,
        <var width="6"/>, <var width="6"/> <m>\rangle</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var>
        <condition number="1" tolerance="0.001">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="0" tolerance="0.001">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="1" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="0.3333" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="0.1111" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="1" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="0" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw1-solutions">
      <p>
        The point <m>P</m> is reached when <m>\ln(t)=0</m>, that is, at
        <m>t_0=1</m>; the other two components vanish there as well.
        Differentiating,

      <md>
        \mathbf{r}\,'(t) = \left\langle \frac{1}{t},\
          \frac{3}{(t+2)^2},\ \ln(t)+1 \right\rangle ,
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        so <m>\mathbf{r}\,'(1)=\left\langle 1,\ \tfrac13,\ 1\right\rangle</m>
        and the tangent line is

      <md>
        \left\langle x,y,z\right\rangle
          = s\left\langle 1,\ \tfrac13,\ 1 \right\rangle ,
        \qquad s \in \R .
      </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- =================================================================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="ex-hw1o-location">
    <title>Where Is the Object After Travelling 15?</title>
    <statement>
      <p>The path of an object is</p>

      <md>
        \mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle 12t,\, 8t^{3/2},\, 3t^2 \right\rangle ,
        \qquad t \ge 0,
      </md>

      <p>
        starting at the origin. Find where it is after travelling a distance
        of <m>15</m>.
      </p>
      <p>Its speed at <m>t=0</m> is <var width="6"/>.</p>
      <p>
        The object has travelled a distance of <m>15</m> at time
        <m>t =</m> <var width="6"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        At that moment it is at the point <m>\langle</m> <var width="6"/>,
        <var width="6"/>, <var width="6"/> <m>\rangle</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <setup>
      <var>
        <condition number="12" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="0" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="1" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
        <condition number="1.25" tolerance="0.005">
          <feedback>
          <p>Incorrect.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="12" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="8" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
      <var>
        <condition number="3" tolerance="0.01">
          <feedback>
          <p>Correct.</p>
        </feedback>
        </condition>
              <condition string=".*">
          <feedback>
                <p>Incorrect.</p>
              </feedback>
        </condition>
      </var>
    </setup>
    <solution component="hw1-solutions">
      <p>
        Differentiating,
        <m>\mathbf{v}(t)=\left\langle 12,\ 12t^{1/2},\ 6t\right\rangle</m>,
        and the speed collapses to a perfect square:

      <md>
        \left\lVert\mathbf{v}(t)\right\rVert
          = \sqrt{144+144t+36t^2}
          = \sqrt{36(t+2)^2} = 6(t+2).
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        Hence the distance travelled by time <m>t</m> is

      <md>
        s(t)=\int_0^{t} 6(\tau+2)\,d\tau = 3t^2+12t ,
      </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        and <m>3t^2+12t=15</m> gives <m>t^2+4t-5=(t+5)(t-1)=0</m>, so
        <m>t=1</m>. The object is then at
        <m>\mathbf{r}(1)=\left\langle 12,\ 8,\ 3\right\rangle</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  </exercises>
</section>
